        },
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Gallery Label",
            "textentryhtml":"In the Andes the last millennium BCE witnessed an explosive increase in art production and a consistent set of visual motifs across a variety of media. Fierce, fanged creatures, blending the qualities of humans and predatory animals, appear in abundance, brandishing long claws. Because the style was traditionally associated with the ritual center of Chavín de Huantar, objects encountered across Peru that share these visual qualities have been described as Chavín style. Artists across Peru, however, adapted the Chavín style to their own contexts and needs.\n",
            "remarks":"AAA2_20-T6-1_CLA_FA.pdf - Day 1 installation"
        }
